          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-acc8280ba5f0\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The highlighted “je t’aime” primarily avows the speaker’s loving emotional state. Its continuation—declared dependence, jealousy, and claims of superior love—reinforces truth-conditional presentation. The surrounding self-positioning as servant and concubine gives the declaration slight relationally enactive force, but the core words do not clearly undertake a commitment. Although emphatic and repeated, the utterance is not represented as an involuntary or reflex-like discharge, so intensity alone does not support E.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is minor uncertainty between P=0 and P=1: the broader speech invokes an asymmetrical lover relationship, but the highlighted declaration itself contains no explicit undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A directly represented spoken declaration by the female speaker, embedded in narration; “reprenait-elle” indicates that she resumes or reiterates it.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The speaker elaborates the declaration through dependence, jealous imaginings, requests for reassurance, self-abasement, and idealizing praise. This makes the avowal rhetorically intense and potentially reassurance-seeking, but those contextual functions do not displace its core presentation of love as true or independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Oh! c’est que je t’aime!”\",\"supports\":\"The causal formulation “c’est que” presents love as an asserted explanation, strongly supporting T; the exclamation supplies intensity but not independently E.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“je t’aime à ne pouvoir me passer de toi”\",\"supports\":\"The repeated declaration and claim that she cannot do without the addressee elaborate the purported loving state, supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“J’ai quelquefois des envies de te revoir où toutes les colères de l’amour me déchirent.”\",\"supports\":\"Longing and the torments of love are offered as evidence of the emotional state being avowed.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je suis ta servante et ta concubine! Tu es mon roi, mon idole!”\",\"supports\":\"The speaker invokes and dramatizes a relational posture, providing limited secondary support for P while primarily contextualizing the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.88},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The declaration is introduced and then repeated with exclamations, but the narration does not say that the words escaped involuntarily or preceded reflection.\",\"supports\":\"Supports E=0 under the narrow calibration: emotional delivery and repetition alone do not establish expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.92}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the dominant avowal, while a low P score records the declaration’s slight role in invoking the lovers’ relationship. Emotional intensity, repetition, and idealization are adequately handled as context and delivery, without E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification applies to the first highlighted occurrence of “je t’aime,” while using the immediately repeated phrase and ensuing speech as contextual evidence.\"}"
        }
